## Goal

**Feature Goal**: Ship Mulligan's deterministic file-ledger extraction as a single pure, deterministic, zero-dependency module (`src/ledger.ts`) that is fully unit-testable in isolation and consumed by the rewind-note renderer, the rewind marker, and the mutation-warning gate. One public function `extractFileLedger(messages, range)` + the `FileLedger`/`MessageLike` types, exactly per spec/04 §2.2, spec/10 §1.6, spec/05 §1 step 5, spec/08 E5/E1/E8/E13, spec/03 §2.3/§7, spec/06 §2–§4.

**Deliverable**:
- REPLACE the `export {};` stub in `src/ledger.ts` with the real implementation: `extractFileLedger`, exported types `FileLedger` + `MessageLike` (+ module-private `ToolCallContent`, `ContentBlock`), tool-name sets (READ/WRITE/BASH), bash classification constants + helpers (read-only whitelist, file-mutator set, redirect/ignore-path regexes, find-destructive regex), and module-private defensive helpers.
- CREATE `test/ledger.test.ts` — the full vitest suite (spec/10 §1.6 pinned contract + empty span + de-dup/sort + readFiles/modifiedFiles/bashSideEffects classification + range-iteration semantics + defensive never-throws + type-level checks).

**Success Definition**: `npx vitest run test/ledger.test.ts` is green and `npx tsc --noEmit` is clean; `src/ledger.ts` imports NOTHING (no Pi, no config, no log, no tokens); the module is pure, deterministic, never throws on adversarial input (throwing-Proxy accessors, circular arguments, non-array content, out-of-bounds/garbage indices), and reproduces the spec/10 §1.6 pinned example EXACTLY.

## Why

- This is the SECOND correctness surface of Milestone M2 (the pure, zero-Pi, fully unit-tested core), the sibling of `tokens.ts` (P1.M2.T1.S1, Complete). It is the **state-ledger** half of the confabulation defense (D/D17): the rewind note tells the resumed model the TRUE filesystem state of the abandoned span so it does not blindly redo side-effectful work (E5).
- spec/05 §1 step 5 + spec/04 §3: the rewind tool persists `ledger: FileLedger` into the `mulligan:rewind` marker and feeds it to `renderNote` (P1.M2.T3) so the note's `<files-read>`/`<files-modified>`/`<bash-side-effects>` blocks render. It also drives `requireMutationWarning` (P1.M4.T1) when `modifiedFiles`/`bashSideEffects` is non-empty.
- The contract is explicit that this must be a **deterministic extraction** (NOT a model call — D2: the note redirects, it does not merely describe). The bash policy is the load-bearing design choice: it is **asymmetric** — `bashSideEffects` is HIGH-RECALL ("when in doubt, include", because a missed write is the dangerous failure E5) while `modifiedFiles`-from-bash is HIGH-PRECISION (only confident parseable paths). This asymmetry is what makes `git commit` land in `bashSideEffects` only (not `modifiedFiles`) — exactly the pinned example.
- Zero-import design: by importing NOTHING, the module is maximally testable and creates no build-order coupling to config/tokens/log. The local `MessageLike` is structurally compatible with a real Pi `AgentMessage[]` (a real list assigns in with no cast).

## What

A single TypeScript module + its test. No Pi surface, no session, no model, no filesystem access, no module-scoped mutable state. The module exposes one pure function:

- `extractFileLedger(messages: MessageLike[] | null | undefined, range: number[] | null | undefined): FileLedger` — iterates `range` (message indices), inspects only `assistant` messages' `toolCall` content blocks, classifies each by tool name into three accumulating Sets, and returns `{ readFiles, modifiedFiles, bashSideEffects }` each de-duplicated + lexicographically sorted.

**Input shape (load-bearing — document it; the rewind tool P1.M4.T1 MUST produce this):** `messages` is the full message list; `range` is a `number[]` of MESSAGE INDICES defining the span (NOT a `[start,end)` tuple). It is the `Unit.indices` array the rewind resolver (P1.M2.T4 transforms) produces, which includes BOTH the assistant message AND its toolResult messages — ledger filters to `role==="assistant"` because only assistant messages carry `toolCall` blocks.

### Success Criteria
- [ ] `src/ledger.ts` exports `extractFileLedger`, `FileLedger`, `MessageLike`.
- [ ] The spec/10 §1.6 PINNED example reproduces EXACTLY: `read(a.ts)+edit(b.ts)+bash(git commit)+bash(ls)` over range `[0,1,2,3]` → `{ readFiles:["a.ts"], modifiedFiles:["b.ts"], bashSideEffects:["git commit"] }` (`ls` omitted as read-only).
- [ ] Empty span (`range []`), empty messages, null messages, null range → `{ readFiles:[], modifiedFiles:[], bashSideEffects:[] }`.
- [ ] readFiles = union of `path ?? file_path` from {read,grep,rg,glob,find,ls}; modifiedFiles = union of `path ?? file_path` from {write,edit} + high-confidence bash write paths; bashSideEffects = non-provably-read-only bash commands verbatim (high-recall).
- [ ] git/node/npm/make → `bashSideEffects` ONLY (their path-like args are scripts/targets, not modified files); `/dev/null`,`/dev/stdout`,… never appear in modifiedFiles.
- [ ] All three lists are de-duplicated and lexicographically sorted (case-sensitive, `[...set].sort()`).
- [ ] NEVER throws on adversarial input (throwing-Proxy accessors, circular arguments, non-array content, non-record blocks, out-of-bounds/non-integer indices). Paths returned VERBATIM (no cwd normalization — pure).
- [ ] `src/ledger.ts` imports NOTHING (verified: no `import` statements at all).
- [ ] `test/ledger.test.ts` is green and covers all 6 tiers (pinned contract, readFiles, modifiedFiles, bashSideEffects, range iteration, defensive never-throws + types).

